<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/paperwork.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/paperwork-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/paperwork.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="680" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span>Claims dictionary</nav><span class="kicker light up">Reference</span><h1 class="up" style="animation-delay:.06s">Property Insurance Claims Dictionary</h1><p class="lede up" style="animation-delay:.14s">40 terms you’ll meet in policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English. Type in the search box to filter.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the dictionary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “holdback” or “ice dam”"></div><div class="az"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-N">N</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-T">T</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-W">W</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></div><dl class="glossary"><dt id="g-A">Actual cash value</dt><dd>The value of damaged property at the time of loss, usually figured as replacement cost minus depreciation. Some policies define it differently.</dd><dt>Additional living expense</dt><dd>Coverage for the extra cost of living elsewhere while a home can’t be lived in after a covered loss, subject to policy limits.</dd><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of a covered loss. It is the question an appraisal panel is normally asked to decide.</dd><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision that sets the amount of loss, generally binding once signed by two of the three panel members.</dd><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy provision that lets either party demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss and sets out how the process works.</dd><dt>Appraisal demand</dt><dd>The written notice from one party invoking the appraisal clause, usually naming that party’s appraiser.</dd><dt id="g-C">Coinsurance</dt><dd>A policy condition that can reduce a claim payment if the property was insured for less than a required percentage of its value. More common on commercial policies.</dd><dt id="g-D">Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing the insured, property, coverages, limits, deductibles and endorsements.</dd><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The portion of a covered loss the policyholder pays before the insurer’s payment begins.</dd><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>A reduction in value for age, wear and condition, subtracted from replacement cost to reach actual cash value.</dd><dt>Derecho</dt><dd>A long-lived, widespread windstorm produced by a line of thunderstorms, capable of straight-line wind damage across a very large area.</dd><dt>Disinterested appraiser</dt><dd>An appraiser with no financial or personal stake in the outcome. Many policies require appraisers to be disinterested, impartial or independent.</dd><dt id="g-E">Endorsement</dt><dd>A written change to a policy that adds, removes or modifies coverage, such as a sewer backup endorsement.</dd><dt>Estimate review</dt><dd>A line-by-line check of an insurance estimate for missing items, incorrect quantities and pricing that doesn’t reflect local costs.</dd><dt>Exclusion</dt><dd>Policy language that removes a cause of loss or type of property from coverage, such as flood or wear and tear.</dd><dt id="g-H">Hail bruising</dt><dd>Damage to a shingle from hail impact that may not be obvious at first glance but can loosen granules and weaken the mat.</dd><dt>Holdback</dt><dd>The portion of a replacement cost claim, usually recoverable depreciation, that the insurer withholds until repairs are completed.</dd><dt id="g-I">Ice and water shield</dt><dd>A self-adhering underlayment installed at eaves, valleys and other vulnerable areas to resist leaks from ice dams and wind-driven rain.</dd><dt>Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice that forms at a roof’s edge when melting snow refreezes, which can force water under shingles and into the home.</dd><dt id="g-L">Line item</dt><dd>A single entry in an estimate describing one task or material with its quantity, unit and price.</dd><dt id="g-M">Matching</dt><dd>The question of whether repaired areas must reasonably match undamaged areas in color, texture or style, and what happens when matching materials aren’t available.</dd><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping a roof or drying out a basement. Most policies require it.</dd><dt id="g-N">National Flood Insurance Program</dt><dd>The federal program that provides most flood insurance, which is separate from a standard homeowners policy.</dd><dt id="g-O">Office of the Commissioner of Insurance</dt><dd>Wisconsin’s insurance regulator, known as OCI, which licenses insurers and agents and handles consumer complaints.</dd><dt>Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs to bring repaired portions of a building up to current building codes, subject to a limit.</dd><dt>Overhead and profit</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charge for business overhead and profit, often a disputed item when several trades are involved.</dd><dt id="g-P">Percentage deductible</dt><dd>A deductible set as a percentage of the dwelling coverage limit rather than a flat dollar amount, often applied to wind or hail losses.</dd><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed that some policies require within a set time after a loss or after the insurer’s request.</dd><dt id="g-R">Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation the insurer will pay back once repairs or replacement are completed, under a replacement cost policy.</dd><dt>Replacement cost value</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without a deduction for depreciation.</dd><dt>Reservation of rights</dt><dd>A letter in which the insurer continues handling a claim while reserving the right to deny coverage later.</dd><dt>Roof square</dt><dd>A unit of roofing measure equal to 100 square feet of roof surface.</dd><dt id="g-S">Scope of loss</dt><dd>The description of what was damaged and what work is needed to repair it, before pricing is applied.</dd><dt>Sewer backup endorsement</dt><dd>An optional addition to a homeowners policy that covers water backing up through sewers or drains, or overflowing from a sump, usually up to a separate limit.</dd><dt>Snow load</dt><dd>The weight of accumulated snow and ice on a roof, which can cause sagging, cracking or collapse when it exceeds what the structure can carry.</dd><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request to add or revise items in an insurance estimate after hidden damage is found or omissions are identified.</dd><dt id="g-T">Test square</dt><dd>A marked 10-by-10-foot area on a roof slope used to count hail hits and assess damage.</dd><dt id="g-U">Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers can’t agree on.</dd><dt id="g-W">Wisconsin Insurance Plan</dt><dd>Wisconsin’s FAIR plan, offering basic property insurance to owners who can’t obtain it in the regular market.</dd><dt id="g-X">Xactimate</dt><dd>A widely used estimating software platform for property insurance claims, with regional price lists for labor and materials.</dd></dl></article>
